course: physics
lesson: 21
title: Mirrors, rainbows and the blue sky
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A mirror is so smooth that light bounces off it neatly, at the same angle it arrived, so the picture stays whole; crumpled foil bounces light every which way and shows no picture at all.
explain: White sunlight is really a mixture of all the colours, and when it passes through a raindrop or a glass prism each colour bends by a slightly different amount, so they fan out into a rainbow.
explain: You only ever see a rainbow with the Sun behind you, and it has no end you can walk to, so there is no pot of gold.
explain: The sky is blue because the tiny bits of the air scatter blue light all over the sky much more than red; at sunset the light crosses so much air that most of the blue is scattered away, leaving orange and red.
explain: Light also bends when it goes from water into air, which is why a straw in your glass looks broken at the surface.
fact: A mirror bounces light back at the same angle it arrives, which keeps the picture clear.
fact: A mirror does not really swap left and right; it swaps front and back, which makes writing look back to front.
fact: Ambulances have their name written backwards on the front so drivers can read it the right way in their mirrors.
fact: The inside curve of a spoon shows you upside down, while its outside curve shows you the right way up and spreads the view out.
fact: Car side mirrors and shop mirrors curve outwards to show a wider view.
fact: A rainbow forms when each raindrop bends sunlight, splits it into colours and bounces it back, and you see it only with the Sun behind you.
fact: A fine spray from a garden hose, with your back to the Sun, can make a little rainbow, because any water drop can split light.
fact: A glass prism bends violet light most and red light least, and Newton showed that a second prism could mix the colours back into white.
fact: The sky is blue because the gas in the air scatters blue light much more than red; from the Moon, with no air, the sky is black even by day.
fact: Sunsets are orange and red because sunlight crosses far more air, and most of the blue is scattered away on the way, and dust in the air, such as after a big volcano erupts, scatters away even more blue.
fact: Clouds are white because their droplets are much bigger than air molecules and scatter all colours equally; thick rain clouds look grey because little light gets through.
fact: A straw in water looks bent because light changes direction as it passes from water into air, and herons aim carefully because a fish is not quite where it looks.
exercise: Why can you see yourself clearly in a mirror? | Its smooth surface bounces light back neatly at the same angle | It stores your picture like a camera | It makes its own light in your shape | It soaks up all the light that hits it
exercise: Why does crumpled foil show no clear picture? | It bounces light in all directions | It soaks up all the light | It is too thin for light | It is the wrong colour for a mirror
exercise: Why does your writing look back to front in a mirror? | The mirror swaps front and back, which looks like left and right swapped | The mirror turns everything upside down | The mirror really swaps left and right | The light gets tired and turns round
exercise: Why is "AMBULANCE" written backwards on the front of an ambulance? | So drivers ahead read it the right way in their mirrors | Because the word is too long to fit on the front otherwise | So that people behind cannot read it | It is a mistake that nobody fixed
exercise: How do you look in the inside bowl of a shiny spoon? | Upside down | The right way up and huge | Exactly as in a flat mirror | You cannot see yourself at all
exercise: Why do car side mirrors curve outwards? | To show a wider view | To make cars look bigger | To keep rain off the glass | To show things upside down
exercise: What makes a rainbow after rain? | Raindrops bend sunlight and split it into its colours | Coloured gas floating in the clouds | Paint washed off the roofs by the rain | The Sun shining out in only seven separate colours of light
exercise: Where must the Sun be for you to see a rainbow? | Behind you | Straight in front of you | Right above your head | Hidden behind the clouds
exercise: Why can't you find a pot of gold at the end of a rainbow? | A rainbow has no end you can reach | The gold is buried too deep | Somebody always gets there first | The rainbow moves to another country
exercise: How can you make a little rainbow in the garden? | Spray a fine mist from a hose with your back to the Sun | Shine a torch on a puddle at night | Pour paint into a bucket of water | Spray water straight up at noon while standing in the shade
exercise: Which colour does a glass prism bend the most? | Violet | Red | Green | Yellow
exercise: What did Newton show with a second prism? | The colours could be mixed back into white light | Prisms make colours out of nothing | Light is made of only three colours | White light cannot be split into colours at all, ever
exercise: Why is the sky blue on a sunny day? | The air scatters blue light much more than red | The sky reflects the blue sea | Space behind the air is blue | The Sun gives out only blue light in the daytime
exercise: Why are sunsets orange and red? | The light crosses so much air that most blue is scattered away | The Sun is cooler in the evening | The Sun is on fire in the evening | Sunsets reflect red deserts
exercise: Why are clouds white rather than blue? | Their big droplets scatter all colours equally | Clouds are made of white smoke | Clouds reflect the white snow on high mountains | Clouds have no water in them
exercise: Why does a straw in a glass of water look bent? | Light changes direction as it passes from water into air | The water really bends the straw | The curved glass twists the straw out of shape as you look | Your eyes cannot see through water
test: Me cousin says the sky's blue cos it reflects the sea. Is that right? | No, the air scatters blue light more than other colours | Yes, the sea paints the sky blue | Yes, and over deserts the sky is yellow | No, the sky is blue because the space behind the air is blue
test: What colour is the daytime sky seen from the Moon? | Black, because there is no air to scatter light | Blue, just like on Earth | Red, like a sunset | White, like a cloud
test: After a big volcano erupts, sunsets around the world look extra red. Why? | Dust in the air scatters away even more of the blue | The volcano heats up the Sun | Glowing light from the hot lava reflects all over the sky | The volcano paints the clouds red
test: In a cartoon a leprechaun hides gold where the rainbow touches the ground. Why could you never get there? | The rainbow moves as you move and has no fixed end | The leprechaun moves it to a new place every night | It is always in another country | The gold is invisible in daylight
test: Why does a heron stab a little beside where the fish appears to be? | Light bending at the surface makes the fish look shifted | Herons have bad eyesight | Fish swim backwards to trick herons | The heron's beak is bent
test: Why do rain clouds look dark grey? | They are so thick that hardly any light gets through | They are full of black dust | They are colder than white clouds | They are reflecting the dark ground and the sea under them
test: What would you see if you looked at the back of a shiny spoon? | Yourself the right way up and spread out | Yourself upside down and much smaller than you | Nothing at all, because the back is too curved | Only the colours of the rainbow, spread out
test: Your mate says a mirror swaps left and right, so why doesn't it swap up and down? What is the real answer? | It swaps front and back, not left and right | It does swap up and down if you wait | Gravity stops it swapping up and down, but not sideways | Mirrors only work sideways
test: Which shows that white light is a mixture of colours? | A prism spreading sunlight into a band of colours | A shadow on the wall | A mirror showing your face | A lamp switched off
test: A shopkeeper puts a big curved mirror in the corner of the shop. Why curved? | To show a wider view of the whole shop | To make the whole shop look much smaller | To make all the goods on sale look shiny | To stop thieves from seeing their own faces
test: You see a rainbow with the Sun low behind you in the evening. Which side of the sky is it in? | The side opposite the Sun | The very same side as the Sun | Straight up overhead, very high | Underneath you, below the ground
exam: Identify the principal cause of the blue colour of the daytime sky. | The preferential scattering of blue light by the gases of the atmosphere | The reflection of the oceans upon the atmosphere | The intrinsic blue colour of outer space | The emission of blue light alone by the Sun
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of a rainbow? | Sunlight is refracted, dispersed and reflected by droplets of water | Coloured vapours are suspended within the clouds | The Sun emits seven discrete beams of colour | Rainbows are formed solely by moonlight
exam: It is given that an observer perceives a rainbow. Determine the position of the Sun relative to the observer. | Behind the observer | Directly before the observer | Directly overhead | Below the horizon in front of the observer
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a straight rod partially immersed in water appears bent. | Light changes direction upon passing from water into air | The rod is deformed by the pressure of the water | The vessel distorts the rod mechanically by means of its curvature | The eye cannot perceive objects beneath water
